# What ships in the tarball is what somebody who installed the package needs in vendor/, and the
# line is drawn at who the file is written for. A consumer reads the README to use it, the upgrade
# guide to move between versions, the changelog to know what moved and the licence because it has
# to be there. A contributor reads the rest, and reads it on the repository rather than out of a
# dependency directory — as does anyone reporting a vulnerability.
#
# Everything under src/ and resources/ ships without exception. src/Testing/ is production code by
# design: it is the contract suite a third-party ledger driver runs against. resources/lang/ is
# what the package prints.
#
# So do the migration guides and stubs/. A guide is written for the reader coming from another
# package, and the Rector stub it tells them to copy has to be there in vendor/ when they do.

/.github export-ignore
/docker export-ignore
/tests export-ignore
/benchmarks export-ignore
/.editorconfig export-ignore
/.gitattributes export-ignore
/.gitignore export-ignore

/CONTRIBUTING.md export-ignore
/SECURITY.md export-ignore

/Makefile export-ignore
/compose.yaml export-ignore
/phpstan.neon.dist export-ignore
/phpunit.xml.dist export-ignore
/pint.json export-ignore
/rector.php export-ignore
/testbench.yaml export-ignore
